From: Fold 3 mainline port
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: add SM8350 (Lahaina) support

SM8350's camera subsystem is Titan 580. For everything mainline camss
touches, 580 is register-identical to SM8250's Titan 480, and Qualcomm's own
GPL drop says so rather than this being inferred:

  * techpack/camera files both under isp_hw/vfe_hw/vfe17x, and cam_vfe580.h
    #includes cam_vfe480.h and then reuses vfe480_top_common_reg,
    vfe480_bus_hw_info and vfe480_top_irq_reg_info verbatim. Only the three
    RDI mux reg_data blocks differ, and camss does not use those.
  * cam_ife_csid17x.c maps compatible "qcom,csid580" to
    cam_ife_csid480_hw_info -- the same struct "qcom,csid480" gets.

So this reuses vfe_ops_480 and csid_ops_gen2 unchanged. Two things do differ
from SM8250 and need code:

1. Topology. SM8350 has 3 full IFEs + 2 IFE-lites (SM8250: 2 + 2), hence 3
   CSIDs + 2 lite CSIDs and 6 CSIPHYs. That breaks the hardcoded `id >= 2`
   test which camss-csid.c uses to pick the lite CSID register offset inside
   the VFE window: on SM8350 the lites are ids 3 and 4. Use the resource's
   own is_lite flag instead, which is correct on SM8250 too.

2. CSIPHY. SM8350 is "qcom,csiphy-v1.2.3" where SM8250 is "v1.2.1", and
   cam_csiphy_soc.c selects a genuinely different register sequence per
   version, so lane_regs_sm8250 cannot be reused. lane_regs_sm8350 is
   generated from the vendor's csiphy_2ph_v1_2_3_reg by
   camera-work/port-csiphy-sm8350.py, which validates the transform by
   replaying it on v1.2.1 and diffing against the in-tree lane_regs_sm8250 --
   all 100 entries reproduce exactly, which is what makes the 95 generated
   ones trustworthy.

Register addresses, interrupts and clock rates all come from the vendor's
lahaina-camera.dtsi and the generated camcc-sm8350 frequency tables, not from
sm8250 by analogy. Note CCI is at 0xac4f000/0xac50000 on SM8350, not the
0xac4a000/0xac4b000 that SM8250 and SC8280XP use.

Tested on a Galaxy Z Fold 3 (SM-F926B, q2q) against the rear ultrawide
IMX258 on CCI1 master 0 / CSIPHY4.

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
index 48459b46a..92d073c53 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss-csid.c
@@ -738,6 +738,7 @@ static int csid_set_power(struct v4l2_subdev *sd, int on)
 		csid->res->hw_ops->hw_version(csid);
 	} else {
 		disable_irq(csid->irq);
+		camss_restore_clock_rates(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
 		camss_disable_clocks(csid->nclocks, csid->clock);
 		regulator_bulk_disable(csid->num_supplies,
 				       csid->supplies);
@@ -1113,12 +1114,13 @@ int msm_csid_subdev_init(struct camss *camss, struct csid_device *csid,
 
 	/* Memory */
 
-	if (camss->res->version == CAMSS_8250) {
-		/* for titan 480, CSID registers are inside the VFE region,
+	if (camss->res->version == CAMSS_8250 ||
+	    camss->res->version == CAMSS_8350) {
+		/* for titan 480/580, CSID registers are inside the VFE region,
 		 * between the VFE "top" and "bus" registers. this requires
 		 * VFE to be initialized before CSID
 		 */
-		if (id >= 2) /* VFE/CSID lite */
+		if (csid->res->is_lite) /* VFE/CSID lite */
 			csid->base = csid->res->parent_dev_ops->get_base_address(camss, id)
 				+ VFE_480_LITE_CSID_OFFSET;
 		else
